  "query_preview": "..A Pacific Northwest storm system is centered near\nthe Idaho/Nevada/Utah triple point this evening, with the\nassociated cold front near a KINS-KEVW line. In a deeply moist and\nunstable airmass over northern Utah, convection was fairly\nwidespread, with some severe cells along the Wasatch Front that\nproduce hail to golf ball size, strong winds, localized urban\nflooding, and even one tornado that impacted Davis and Morgan\ncounties. Late this evening, convection is weaker and more\nlocalized, with the strongest showers continuing over the frontal\nboundary. \n\nConvection will tend to diminish during the overnight hours as \none lobe of the low exits the area, with another expected to \nrotate in by Saturday afternoon. The trough is expected to track \nsteadily east during the day Saturday, centered near the \nUtah/Idaho border. Thus, the focus of convection will move \neastward during the day. West to northwest flow behind the low \nwill continue to draw drier air in front the west, steadily \ndecreasing the threat of convection. Behind the front, \ntemperatures for northern Utah are expected to run up to 10F below\nseasonal normals, with southern Utah temperatures staying closer \nto normal near the front. \n\nHigh pressure is expected to move overhead over Utah and southwest\nWyoming on Sunday in between the exiting trough and another broad\nlow approaching the California coast. Guidance is in good\nagreement that the California low will track eastward into the\nGreat Basin by Tuesday, bringing a return of moisture and\ninstability to the area. There still remains a good amount of\ndisagreement in the guidance, however, with regard to the precise\ntrack of the system and how much it weakens as it moves onshore.\nThus, it is currently difficult to determine the areas that might\nsee more significant convection over the area by midweek, though\nthe ensembles are leaning modestly toward southern and eastern\nUtah. The track of the low should have little impact on\ntemperatures, which should stay in the vicinity of seasonal\nnormals for much of the upcoming week.",
